      "id": "355uj5d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Sources of Information about Earth's Interior"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Direct sources of information about Earth's interior include surface rock and deep mining, while indirect sources include seismic waves, gravity, magnetic fields, and meteors.",
        "P-waves (Primary) are longitudinal waves that travel through solids, liquids, and gases, whereas S-waves (Secondary) are transverse waves that travel only through solids.",
        "The Crust is the outermost solid shell, consisting of thinner, denser basaltic Oceanic Crust and thicker, less dense granitic Continental Crust.",
        "The Mantle extends to a depth of 2,900 km; its upper portion, the asthenosphere, is the primary source of magma.",
        "The Core is composed mostly of heavy materials like nickel and iron (NIFE), featuring a liquid outer core and a solid inner core."
      ],
      "id": "lfdbvlx"
    },
